STEP 1 — Create the Unity project

Open Unity Hub.

Create a new project using the 2D template.

Use:

Project Name: Crystal Quest

Use the current Unity LTS version available in your Unity Hub.

Then create the project.

STEP 2 — Import Kenney's assets

After Unity opens, create this folder structure inside Assets:

Assets
├── Art
├── Audio
├── Prefabs
├── Scenes
├── Scripts
├── VisualScripts
└── UI

Since we're using Visual Scripting, Scripts won't be our main folder, but I want it there so the project remains organized if we later need Unity components or small supporting scripts.

Now take the downloaded Platformer Art Deluxe folder and put it inside:

Assets/Art/PlatformerArtDeluxe

Let Unity finish importing everything.

Don't start dragging random assets into the scene yet.

STEP 3 — Create our first scene

Inside:

Assets/Scenes

Create/save a new scene as:

Level_01_LostIsland

Your project should now roughly look like:

Assets
│
├── Art
│   └── PlatformerArtDeluxe
│
├── Audio
│
├── Prefabs
│
├── Scenes
│   └── Level_01_LostIsland
│
├── Scripts
│
├── UI
│
└── VisualScripts
STEP 4 — Set up the 2D world

Before creating the player, let's make our first little piece of level.

Find the ground/platform tiles from the Kenney pack.

Drag a suitable ground asset into the Scene.

Create something simple:

                         🌳
                ┌─────────────┐
                │             │
        ┌───────┘             │
        │                     │
────────┴─────────────────────┴────────
              GROUND

Don't worry about making it beautiful yet.

We're only proving that:

Our game can have a playable physical world.

STEP 5 — Add physics to the ground

This is our first important Unity concept.

Select the ground.

Add:

Box Collider 2D

The collider tells Unity:

"This object physically exists."

Without it, our player will simply fall through the artwork.

Sprite ≠ Physical Object

A sprite is just the visual.

A collider gives it a physical boundary.

STEP 6 — Create the Player

Now find a character from the Kenney pack.

Drag the character into the scene.

Rename the GameObject:

Player

Add:

Rigidbody 2D

Then add:

Capsule Collider 2D
(or another collider that fits the character).

Now our Player has:

Player
│
├── Sprite Renderer
├── Rigidbody 2D
└── Collider 2D

This is the beginning of our actual game object.

STEP 7 — Freeze the player's rotation

Select:

Player → Rigidbody 2D → Constraints

Freeze:

Rotation Z

Otherwise the character can fall over when it hits things.
